    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Naples began as the Greek colony of Parthenope in the 8th century BCE and was refounded as Neapolis, and it kept its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and customs after it came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</li><li>It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-naples">Duchy of Naples</a>, then of the Kingdom of Naples from 1282, and finally of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-unification">Italian unification</a> in 1861.</li><li>Under Spanish rule it became one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> art, until a plague in 1656 killed about half of its inhabitants.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1943 the people of Naples rose against the German occupation, and Colonel Scholl, the German commander, negotiated a surrender to the insurgents; the city was already free when British and American forces entered it on 1 October.</p></aside>
    </section>

