    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">8th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from Cumae found Parthenope on the Pizzofalcone hill</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">507 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The city is refounded as Neapolis by Cumaean aristocrats</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">326 BCE</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> besieges Neapolis, which surrenders after a siege of a year</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">536</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> takes the city for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> by entering through an aqueduct</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1137</span><span class="rf-text">The last duke surrenders to Roger II and Naples joins the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1266</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> moves the capital of the kingdom from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> to Naples</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1656</span><span class="rf-text">A plague kills about half of the city's inhabitants</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1861</span><span class="rf-text">Naples becomes part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/odysseus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/odysseus.jpg" alt="Odysseus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Head_Odysseus_MAR_Sperlonga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jastrow, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Naples stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Gulf of Naples</a> between two volcanic regions, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-vesuvius">Mount Vesuvius</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campi-flegrei">Phlegraean Fields</a>. The area has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period, and traces of the Serra d'Alto culture were found near the basilica of Santa Maria degli Angeli a Pizzofalcone, with later finds from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcolithic">Chalcolithic</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. In the second millennium BCE a first Mycenaean settlement arose not far from the site of the future Parthenope.</p><p>Sailors from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> probably founded a small commercial port called Parthenope on the island of Megaride in the ninth century BCE. Cumae, the earliest Greek city on mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, expanded the settlement in the 8th century BCE to include the Pizzofalcone promontory and Monte Echia, where it could control sea traffic. It was named after a siren of Greek mythology who was said to have washed ashore at Megaride after failing to bewitch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Ulysses</a>. Little archaeology of Parthenope has come to light, though a necropolis of the 7th century BCE was found in via Nicotera.</p><p>The city was refounded as Neapolis, the New City, in the 6th century BCE, and one account dates this to 507 BCE, by the Cumaean aristocracy expelled by the tyrant Aristodemus. The oligarchs planned a second Cumae and kept its cults and its organisation. The old settlement became Palaipolis, the old city. Neapolis was laid out on a rectangular grid on a plateau, and swamps limited its farmland and turned it towards the sea and trade.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/claudius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/claudius.jpg" alt="Claudius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Claudius_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neapolis became one of the foremost cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a>. Its trade grew as the tyranny of the Deinomenids at Syracuse declined after 466 BCE, and it occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ischia">Ischia</a> when the Syracusan garrison left. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> built a network of trade in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>, to the benefit of the port, until the Athenian expedition against Syracuse failed in 413 BCE and ties weakened. At the end of the 5th century BCE Neapolis admitted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> elites to public office to keep its independence from the Samnites, and it took in many refugees from Cumae after that city fell in 421 BCE.</p><p>Under Roman rule the city kept its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a>, institutions and customs and was expanded with villas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aqueduct">aqueducts</a> and public baths. From 199 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pozzuoli">Puteoli</a> replaced it as a maritime power, and it declined further after it backed the losing side in the civil wars. Under Augustus it recovered as a centre of Hellenistic culture, and in 2 CE he founded the Isolympic Games there, which he attended in 14 CE. Emperors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> holidayed in the area, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> studied there.</p><p>Under Spanish rule Naples became one of the largest cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>, home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>, Salvator Rosa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-lorenzo-bernini">Bernini</a> and to the philosophers Telesio, Bruno, Campanella and Vico. Pedro Álvarez de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, the most important of the viceroys, paved roads, strengthened and expanded the walls and put up fortresses and buildings. After the cholera of 1884 the city was rebuilt in part under the risanamento, which gave it the Galleria <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umberto-i">Umberto I</a> and the Corso Umberto.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus.jpg" alt="Romulus Augustulus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gold_Solidus_of_Romulus_Augustulus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ANS, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neapolis was an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and was taken by the Samnites before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> captured it. In 326 BCE the consul Publilius Philo besieged it, and after a siege of a year it surrendered through betrayal by Greek citizens. It became a Roman municipium and held Roman rule until the fifth century, when the last western emperor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus-augustulus">Romulus Augustulus</a>, was exiled to a villa on the islet of Megaride. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> held the city until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> took it for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 536. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> took it in 543 and treated it leniently, and it later came under imperial rule again.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-naples">Duchy of Naples</a> arose under Byzantine authority, and a local duke, Basilius, ruled it from 661. Duke Stephen II turned its allegiance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to the Pope, and the duchy was independent in practice from 840. Sergius VII surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-ii-of-sicily">Roger II of Sicily</a> in 1137, and Naples joined the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>, under Henry VI, conquered the kingdom in 1194, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> made Naples its capital in 1266.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> the Angevins kept the mainland as the Kingdom of Naples. Alfonso of Aragon conquered the city in 1442. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> won it from the French in 1503 and ruled it through viceroys until 1714, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> took it. In 1734 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain">Charles of Bourbon</a> made it the capital of an independent kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> conquered it in 1806, and his brother Joseph and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> reigned there. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> returned in 1815, the kingdom became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-garibaldi">Garibaldi</a>'s forces ended its rule in 1860.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Naples&#8221; cites where it covers Greek birth and Roman acquisition, As part of the Kingdom of Sicily, Kingdom of Naples:</p><ul><li>Tommaso Astarita, Introduction: &quot;Naples is the whole world&quot; (Brill, 2013)</li><li>Cordelia Warr, Art and Architecture in Naples, 1266–1713: New Approaches (John Wiley &amp; Sons, 2010)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo.jpg" alt="Teatro di San Carlo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo">Teatro di San Carlo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teatro_de_San_Carlos,_N%C3%A1poles,_Italia,_2023-03-25,_DD_184-186_HDR.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Diego Delso, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Neapolis kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> and customs of its founders long after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> absorbed it. It had an acropolis, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a>, strong walls, an odeon, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and a temple of its patron gods, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castor-and-pollux">Dioscuri</a>, and its grid of streets can still be seen in the historic centre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> came in the Roman period. According to tradition the basilica of San Pietro ad Aram stands where Saint Peter preached and baptised the first converts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/januarius">Januarius</a>, the city's patron saint, was martyred in 305 near Naples. Each year the Cathedral hosts the Miracle of Saint Januarius, in which his dried blood is said to turn liquid.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the duchy was ruled by an aristocracy of warriors and landowners, and in the 12th century commerce was left mainly to foreigners from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. Under the Angevins, Pisan and Genoese merchants and Tuscan bankers came, and artists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Boccaccio</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto</a>. Frederick II founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-naples-federico-ii">University of Naples</a> in 1224. The Naples of the early 19th century was divided between the lazzaroni, a mass of very poor and devout people who favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>, a royal bureaucracy and an elite of landowners.</p><p>The city also developed a tradition of music and theatre. The first music conservatories were founded under Spanish rule in the 16th century, opera buffa was created there in the 18th century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teatro-di-san-carlo">Teatro di San Carlo</a>, built in 1737, has been called the oldest working theatre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Neapolitan language is regarded as distinct from Italian, and Alfonso V stated in 1442 that it should be used in official documents in place of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/belisarius"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belisarius.jpg" alt="Belisarius" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Belisarius_mosaic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Naples was besieged often. In 1191 the future Henry VI laid siege to it from May to August, and disease forced his army to withdraw. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gothic-war-535-554">Gothic War</a> brought the siege of 536, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a>'s troops entered by the aqueduct, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/totila">Totila</a> starved the city into surrender in 543. In 1544 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs took around 7,000 people from Naples as slaves.</p><p>The pressures of a crowded city exploded in July 1647 when the fisherman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaniello">Masaniello</a> led a revolt against Spanish rule. Neapolitans declared a republic and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for support, but the Spaniards put down the rising in April 1648. In 1656 an outbreak of bubonic plague killed about half of the city's inhabitants and began a period of decline. In 1799 French troops entered the city and a Parthenopaean Republic was proclaimed, but the royalist lazzaroni rose against it, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> arrested its leaders in June and handed them over for execution.</p><p>Disease struck in the 19th century. Twelve epidemics of cholera and typhoid fever killed some 48,000 people between 1834 and 1884, and a major cholera epidemic in August 1884, blamed largely on poor sewers, led to the risanamento. Between 1876 and 1913 an estimated 4 million people emigrated from the area. Naples was the most-bombed Italian city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. In the Four Days of Naples the people rose against the German occupation, and the departing Germans burned the university's library, destroyed the city archives and sank more than 300 ships in the harbour.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo.jpg" alt="Castel Nuovo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Municip.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Little john at Italian Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Naples left its mark in art, learning and music. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-naples-federico-ii">University of Naples Federico II</a>, founded in 1224, is regarded as the oldest state-funded secular university in the world, and L'Orientale, founded in 1732 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> Matteo Ripa, is the oldest European institution for Oriental studies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>'s arrival in 1606 was a critical factor in the Neapolitan school of painting. The city was also a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, and its song tradition produced pieces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà</a>.</p><p>The city's buildings record its successive rulers: the Castel dell'Ovo, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-nuovo">Castel Nuovo</a> built under Charles I, Castel Sant'Elmo completed in 1329, the Royal Palace and the Piazza del Plebiscito begun by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> and finished under Ferdinand IV. The rebuilding of the church of Santa Chiara, destroyed in an American bombing raid, became the symbol of the city's rebirth after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Beneath the city lie tunnels and cisterns dug out of the soft tuff on which much of it is built.</p><p>Naples is today the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a> and the largest city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the third largest in Italy after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, with 905,050 residents within its boundaries. Its population had peaked at 1,226,594 in 1971. Its historic centre was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 1995, and the rectangular grid of the Greek foundation can still be traced in it.</p></div><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae<span>c. 1250</span></a><a href="/songs/lhomme-arme">L'homme armé (The Armed Man)<span>c. 1453</span></a><a href="/songs/santa-lucia">Santa Lucia<span>1849</span></a><a href="/songs/funiculi-funicula">Funiculì, Funiculà<span>1880</span></a><a href="/songs/o-sole-mio">'O sole mio<span>1898</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Naples, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-naples">Gulf of Naples</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, began as the Greek colony of Parthenope and was refounded as Neapolis. It passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to Byzantine, Norman, Angevin, Aragonese, Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> rulers and was the capital of the Kingdom of Naples until 1861.</em></p>

